{"tool_name":"jellyfish","contents":["biocontainers","galaxy","bioschemas","debian","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"jellyfish","identifiers":["biotools:jellyfish","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btr011"],"license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"A command-line algorithm for counting k-mers in dna sequence.","home_url":"https://github.com/gmarcais/Jellyfish","keywords":["Animals","Base Sequence","Computational Biology","Genome","Humans","Sequence Alignment","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Sequence analysis","Genomics"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":5322},"biotools":{"id":"jellyfish","home":"http://www.genome.umd.edu/jellyfish.html","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"A command-line algorithm for counting k-mers in DNA sequence.","addition_date":"2017-01-13T13:15:10Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:35:36.609714Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["2.3.0"],"collections":["Animal and Crop Genomics"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:jellyfish","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"carlk@cs.cmu.edu","name":"Carl Kingsford","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"gmarcais@umd.edu","name":"Guillaume Mar\u00e7ais","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/gmarcais/Jellyfish/tree/develop/swig"},{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://www.genome.umd.edu/docs/JellyfishUserGuide.pdf"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["ELIXIR-EE","animalandcropgenomics","jw"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence trace","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0924"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"},{"term":"FASTQ","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1930"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"k-mer counting","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3472"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Count matrix","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3917"},"format":[{"term":"K-mer countgraph","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3665"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Jellyfish","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"seqwiki_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btr011","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: Counting the number of occurrences of every k-mer (substring of length k) in a long string is a central subproblem in many applications, including genome assembly, error correction of sequencing reads, fast multiple sequence alignment and repeat detection. Recently, the deep sequence coverage generated by next-generation sequencing technologies has caused the amount of sequence to be processed during a genome project to grow rapidly, and has rendered current k-mer counting tools too slow and memory intensive. At the same time, large multicore computers have become commonplace in research facilities allowing for a new parallel computational paradigm. Results: We propose a new k-mer counting algorithm and associated implementation, called Jellyfish, which is fast and memory efficient. It is based on a multithreaded, lock-free hash table optimized for counting k-mers up to 31 bases in length. Due to their flexibility, suffix arrays have been the data structure of choice for solving many string problems. For the task of k-mer counting, important in many biological applications, Jellyfish offers a much faster and more memory-efficient solution. \u00a9 The Author 2011. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved.","authors":[{"name":"Kingsford C."},{"name":"Marcais G."}],"citationCount":2647,"date":"2011-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"A fast, lock-free approach for efficient parallel counting of occurrences of k-mers"},"pmcid":"PMC3051319","pmid":"21217122"}],"topic":[{"term":"Genomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0622"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Jellyfish","home":"https://bio.tools/jellyfish","license":"GPL-3.0","version":"2.3.0","su